      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Prolonged survival of eosinophils plays an important role in the pathogenesis of Churg-Strauss syndrome (CSS); however, its detailed molecular mechanism is still unclear. TRAIL and its receptors are expressed on a variety of cells, including eosinophils. In this study, we examined the expression of TRAIL receptors on eosinophils from patients with CSS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "TRAIL receptor expression was assessed on eosinophils from healthy volunteers, patients with CSS, patients with asthma, and patients with hypereosinophilia due to parasitic infection. TRAIL-induced apoptosis of eosinophils was compared between the patients with CSS and patients with asthma. RNA interference was used to assess the effects of suppression of TRAIL receptor 3."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Expression of TRAIL receptor 3, a decoy receptor that acts as an antiapoptotic receptor, on eosinophils from patients with CSS was significantly higher than that in the other subjects. Moreover, in CSS, serum TRAIL receptor 3 levels showed a significant positive correlation with peripheral eosinophil counts, tissue-infiltrating eosinophils stained positive for this receptor, and peripheral T cells expressed TRAIL on their surface. Compared with asthma patients, eosinophils from CSS patients showed a significantly lower percentage of recombinant TRAIL, less autologous T cell-induced apoptosis, and decreased level of active caspase 3. Suppression of TRAIL receptor 3 through RNA interference significantly increased the recombinant TRAIL-induced apoptosis of eosinophils from CSS patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Increased expression of TRAIL receptor 3 on eosinophils from patients with CSS was observed. These alterations in TRAIL receptor 3 expression might be involved in the molecular pathogenesis of CSS eosinophilia."
        }
      ]},
